2026-04-03 00:52:09 - Agent_20260403004820 - INFO - [*] Tool call: complete_review
2026-04-03 00:52:09 - Agent_20260403004820 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed 9 CodeQL queries identifying OS command injection vulnerabilities in plexus-utils Commandline/BourneShell classes. Confirmed 4 true positive findings:

1. **BourneShell.getExecutionPreamble() (line 118)** [CRITICAL]: Working directory passed through `unifyQuotes()` which wraps in double-quotes when trigger chars ($, backtick etc.) are present, but only escapes the double-quote itself. `$()` and backtick command substitution are evaluated by bash inside double-quoted strings. This is the primary injection vector.

2. **BourneShell.getExecutable() (line 71)** [HIGH]: Executable path also passes through `unifyQuotes()` with the same double-quote wrapping vulnerability — `$()` and backticks in executable paths are executed by bash.

3. **BourneShell.unifyQuotes() first branch (line 157)** [HIGH]: When path has no space, contains a single-quote, and no double-quote, `StringUtils.escape()` is used which only escapes `'`, `"`, `\` — all other shell metacharacters (`$`, `` ` ``, `&&`, `;`, `|`) pass through unescaped and unquoted into the shell command string.

4. **Commandline.execute() (line 636/651)** [CRITICAL]: The actual exec sink — `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(getShellCommandline())` uses array form but the 3rd element is a full shell command string passed to `/bin/sh -c`, so BourneShell's incomplete escaping of working directory and executable paths leads to command injection at this point.

All findings trace through the complete dataflow: user-controlled working directory → Shell.setWorkingDirectory → BourneShell.getExecutionPreamble → unifyQuotes (incomplete escaping) → shell command string → Runtime.exec → /bin/sh -c (shell evaluation).
